<!-- ================== SUMMARY AND GOALS ================== -->
<section class="band" id="research">
  <div class="band__inner">
    <p class="eyebrow" data-reveal>The group</p>
    <h2 class="head" data-reveal data-delay="1">We work on what gravitational waves can tell us about matter, gravity and the early Universe.</h2>

    <div class="lead" data-reveal data-delay="2">
      <p>Gravitational waves are ripples in the curvature of spacetime, produced when compact massive objects accelerate or collide, and travelling to us at the speed of light. Measuring these tiny distortions lets us probe gravity in its strongest regime, test general relativity where it is hardest to test, and study events that leave no trace in light.</p>
      <p>Our group is the gravitational-wave strand of the <strong>IFJ PAN Astrophysics Forum</strong>, working alongside the Institute's cosmic-ray, neutrino and gamma-ray programmes. Researchers based in <strong>NZ15</strong> take part in the science programme of the <strong>Einstein Telescope</strong>, Europe's third-generation underground observatory, contributing theory, numerical relativity, waveform modelling, data-analysis methods and the multi-messenger framework that ties gravitational waves to neutrino and cosmic-ray observations.</p>
      <p>We are a small, mixed group of PhD students, postdocs and senior researchers, and we are open about it: much of what follows is being built now, and new people shape it.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="goals">
      <div class="goal" data-reveal>
        <p class="goal__tag">Dense matter</p>
        <div class="goal__body">
          <h3>Neutron stars as gravitational-wave sources</h3>
          <p>Connect equations of state for matter at supranuclear density to observable waveforms — from oscillations of compact stars to the moments around a merger.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="goal" data-reveal>
        <p class="goal__tag">Early Universe</p>
        <div class="goal__body">
          <h3>Signals from the first moments</h3>
          <p>Model cosmological phase transitions, cosmic strings, primordial black holes and quantum-gravity effects, and derive the gravitational-wave background they would leave behind.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="goal" data-reveal>
        <p class="goal__tag">Waveforms</p>
        <div class="goal__body">
          <h3>The parts of the signal that get idealised away</h3>
          <p>Gravitational waves from eccentric binaries and the influence of spin-induced effects — physics that the standard circular, aligned-spin picture misses.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="goal" data-reveal>
        <p class="goal__tag">Inference</p>
        <div class="goal__body">
          <h3>How well will we actually measure it?</h3>
          <p>Data-analysis methods and Fisher-matrix techniques to assess parameter-estimation accuracy for future Einstein Telescope observations, at the event rates a third-generation detector implies.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="goal" data-reveal>
        <p class="goal__tag">Multi-messenger</p>
        <div class="goal__body">
          <h3>One event, several messengers</h3>
          <p>Develop the framework linking gravitational-wave observations with neutrinos and cosmic rays, drawing on the Institute's long-running work in both.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="goal" data-reveal>
        <p class="goal__tag">Next</p>
        <div class="goal__body">
          <h3>Growing towards the instrument</h3>
          <p>In the coming years the team plans to add experimental expertise, strengthening the link between detector technology and astrophysical data analysis.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
